/-! # Invariant normalized blowup dominating an orbit of blowups

Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tags 02ND, 02NS and 0806; Morphisms, Tags 035S and 087S;
de Jong, Families of curves and alterations (1997), Section 2.10.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-invariant-normalized-blowup-domination).

The orbit product and invariant open are specified by equalities. The normal scheme is
identified over the ordinary blowup with its existing normalization in the function field.
Group actions use automorphisms and allow nontrivial action on the ground field.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

open CategoryTheory TopologicalSpace
open scoped BigOperators

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Normalization of the blowup of the orbit product canonically dominates every translated
blowup and carries the compatible semilinear action.

Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tags 02ND, 02NS, 0806; Morphisms, Tags 035S, 035Q, 087S,
01WE and 0B3I; de Jong (1997), Section 2.10.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-invariant-normalized-blowup-domination).

Proof:
1. Every translate $h(U_0)$ is a dense open of the integral scheme $S_0$. Their finite
   intersection $U$ contains the generic point and is dense; $g(U)=\bigcap_h(gh)(U_0)=U$. By the
   affine-local definition of ideal products, $J$ is quasi-coherent: locally its generators are
   the finitely many products of generators of the finitely many factors. Thus it is of finite
   type. On $U$ every factor is the unit ideal, so $J|_U=\mathcal O_U$ and $J$ is nonzero.
   Transport preserves ideal multiplication, and reindexing $h\mapsto gh$ gives $g(J)=J$. No
   faithfulness or $k$-linearity is used. Uses: def-pc-integral-scheme,
   def-sbc-ideal-sheaf-powers, pqf-def-coherent.
2. Apply [lem-sfb-integral-blowup-projective-geometry] to the nonzero finite-type quasi-coherent
   ideal $J$ on the integral scheme $S_0$. It gives that $B$ is integral and $b:B\to S_0$ is
   projective in the projective-bundle sense. By [lem-trf-projective-bundle-proper], $b$ is
   proper, hence of finite type by [def-weil-proper-morphism]. Since $S_0\to\operatorname{Spec}k$
   is of finite type, [lem-sfb-finite-type-composition-base-change](1) shows that $B$ is of finite
   type over $k$. Uses: lem-sfb-integral-blowup-projective-geometry,
   lem-trf-projective-bundle-proper, def-weil-proper-morphism,
   lem-sfb-finite-type-composition-base-change.
3. Because $J|_U=\mathcal O_U$, the open $U$ lies in $S_0\setminus V(J)$. Restrict
   [thm-nagata-global-blowup-complement] further to $U$ to identify $b^{-1}(U)\to U$ with the
   identity. This is a nonempty open in the integral scheme $B$, hence dense. Thus $b$ is
   birational in the stated dense-open sense. Computing rational functions on this common dense
   open, using [def-sc-function-field], identifies $k(B)=k(S_0)$ canonically; call this field $K$.
   Uses: thm-nagata-global-blowup-complement, def-pc-integral-scheme, def-sc-function-field.
4. Fix $h\in H$ and put $J_h=\prod_{g\ne h}g(I)$, taking the empty product to be the unit ideal.
   Then $J=h(I)J_h$. Extension of ideals along $b$ preserves this identity: locally both sides are
   generated by the same products of images of sections. By
   [thm-nagata-blowup-principalizes-center], $J\mathcal O_B$ is an effective Cartier ideal. Apply
   [lemma-nagata-invertible-product-factor] on $B$ to $(h(I)\mathcal O_B)(J_h\mathcal O_B)$: each
   factor is effective Cartier and hence invertible. The universal property
   [thm-nagata-blowup-universal-property] now gives the unique $S_0$-morphism
   $d_h:B\to\operatorname{Bl}_{h(I)}S_0$, using the canonical quotient $b^*h(I)\twoheadrightarrow
   h(I)\mathcal O_B$. This quotient is the image inside $\mathcal O_B$, so there is no choice of a
   line quotient. In particular $d_1$ maps to $V$. Uses: thm-nagata-blowup-principalizes-center,
   lemma-nagata-invertible-product-factor, thm-nagata-blowup-universal-property.
5. For $h\in H$, regard $B$ as an $S_0$-scheme by $h\circ b$. Since $h(J)=J$, the extended ideal
   of $J$ for this map is again $J\mathcal O_B$, invertible by
   [thm-nagata-blowup-principalizes-center]. Apply [thm-nagata-blowup-universal-property] with the
   canonical image quotient to obtain a unique map $\beta_h:B\to B$ satisfying $b\beta_h=hb$. The
   composites $\beta_g\beta_h$ and $\beta_{gh}$ are maps over the same map $ghb$ and induce the
   same canonical quotient onto $J\mathcal O_B$; uniqueness gives $\beta_g\beta_h=\beta_{gh}$.
   Likewise $\beta_1=1$, so $\beta_{h^{-1}}$ is the inverse of $\beta_h$. These maps form the
   required action on $B$. Their compatibility with the original action on $k$ follows by
   composing $b\beta_h=hb$ with the structure map of $S_0$. Uses:
   thm-nagata-blowup-principalizes-center, thm-nagata-blowup-universal-property.
6. Apply [lem-sfb-finite-normalization-model-functoriality] with $V=B$, $H=k(B)=K$ (the letter $H$
   in that node denotes a field), and the degree-one finite extension $M=K$. Its normalization is
   exactly $S=B^\nu$ of [def-sc-normalization]. It is normal integral of finite type over $k$, and
   $\nu$ is finite with $k(S)=K$. The theorem [lem-sc-normalization-exists] applied to the
   integral finite-type $k$-scheme $B$ gives birationality of $\nu$. The open $b^{-1}(U)$ is
   normal because it is isomorphic to the open $U$ of the normal scheme $S_0$. Consequently
   [lem-sc-normalization-over-normal-locus] gives that $\nu$ is an isomorphism over $b^{-1}(U)$.
   Thus $S\to S_0$ is an isomorphism over $U$, and both inverse images are nonempty dense opens of
   integral schemes. This proves the claimed birationality and identifies $k(S)=k(S_0)$. Uses:
   lem-sfb-finite-normalization-model-functoriality, def-sc-normalization,
   lem-sc-normalization-exists, lem-sc-normalization-over-normal-locus.
7. Use the isomorphism-functoriality clause of [lem-sfb-finite-normalization-model-functoriality]
   for the action $\beta_h$ on $B$ and the compatible left field action $A_h=(\beta_{h^{-1}})^*$
   on $K=k(B)$. It supplies a unique semilinear lift to $S$, preserving identities and
   composition. Hence these lifts form an $H$-action and $\nu$ is equivariant. The preceding
   action on $B$ makes $b$ equivariant, so the composite is equivariant as well. Compatibility
   with the action on $k$ is included in that clause; no assumption that $h$ fixes $k$ or acts
   faithfully is needed. Finally $d_1\circ\nu:S\to V$ is an $S_0$-morphism because both $d_1$ and
   $\nu$ are maps over $S_0$. Uses: lem-sfb-finite-normalization-model-functoriality.
8. The given projective embedding $S_0\hookrightarrow\mathbf P^n_k$ supplies an ample invertible
   sheaf by [lem-weil-pbc-projective-ample-invertible]. Apply
   [lem-trf-projective-bundle-to-h-projective] to $b$, which is projective in the
   projective-bundle sense by Step 2: thus $b$ is H-projective. Compose with the given
   H-projective structure map of $S_0$ using [lem-trf-h-projective-composition]; $B/k$ is
   H-projective. Applying [lem-weil-pbc-projective-ample-invertible] again, now to this embedding
   of $B$, gives an ample invertible sheaf on $B$. Since $\nu$ is finite,
   [lem-trf-finite-projective-bundle] makes it projective in the projective-bundle sense, and
   [lem-trf-projective-bundle-to-h-projective] makes it H-projective. Two further applications of
   [lem-trf-h-projective-composition] give that $S\to S_0$ and $S\to\operatorname{Spec}k$ are
   H-projective. Together with Steps 1--7, this proves every assertion, with H-projective
   understood as the finite-projective-space convention, not an equivariant embedding condition.
   Uses: lem-weil-pbc-projective-ample-invertible, lem-trf-projective-bundle-to-h-projective,
   lem-trf-h-projective-composition, lem-trf-finite-projective-bundle.
-/
theorem exists_invariant_normalized_blowup_domination
    (k : Type u) [Field k] (S₀ : Scheme.{u}) (p : S₀ ⟶ Spec (.of k))
    [IsIntegral S₀] [IsHProjective p] [LocallyOfFiniteType p] [QuasiCompact p]
    (hnormal : ∀ x : S₀, IsDomain (S₀.presheaf.stalk x) ∧
      IsIntegrallyClosed (S₀.presheaf.stalk x))
    (H : Type v) [Group H] [Fintype H]
    (σk : H →* (k ≃+* k)) (σ₀ : H →* Aut S₀)
    (hsemilinear : ∀ h : H, (σ₀ h).hom ≫ p =
      p ≫ Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (σk h⁻¹).toRingHom))
    (I : S₀.IdealSheafData) (hfinite : ∀ W : S₀.affineOpens, (I.ideal W).FG)
    (U₀ : S₀.Opens) (hdense : Dense (U₀ : Set S₀)) (hunit : I.comap U₀.ι = ⊤) :
    ∃ (U : S₀.Opens) (J : S₀.IdealSheafData),
      U = (⨅ h : H, (σ₀ h).inv ⁻¹ᵁ U₀) ∧
      J = (∏ h : H, I.comap (σ₀ h).inv) ∧
      Dense (U : Set S₀) ∧
      (∀ h : H, (σ₀ h).hom '' (U : Set S₀) = (U : Set S₀)) ∧
      IsProjectiveBundle J.blowupMap ∧
      (∀ h : H, ∃! d : J.blowup ⟶ (I.comap (σ₀ h).inv).blowup,
        d ≫ (I.comap (σ₀ h).inv).blowupMap = J.blowupMap) ∧
      ∃ (hB : IsIntegral J.blowup) (S : Scheme.{u}) (_ : IsIntegral S)
        (ν : S ⟶ J.blowup) (e : S ≅ @Scheme.normalization J.blowup hB),
        e.hom ≫ @Scheme.fromNormalization J.blowup hB = ν ∧
        IsFinite ν ∧
        (∃ W : J.blowup.Opens, Dense (W : Set J.blowup) ∧
          Dense ((ν ⁻¹ᵁ W : S.Opens) : Set S) ∧ IsIso (ν ∣_ W)) ∧
        (∀ s : S, IsDomain (S.presheaf.stalk s) ∧
          IsIntegrallyClosed (S.presheaf.stalk s)) ∧
        IsHProjective (ν ≫ J.blowupMap ≫ p) ∧
        LocallyOfFiniteType (ν ≫ J.blowupMap ≫ p) ∧
        QuasiCompact (ν ≫ J.blowupMap ≫ p) ∧
        IsHProjective (ν ≫ J.blowupMap) ∧
        IsIso ((ν ≫ J.blowupMap) ∣_ U) ∧
        Dense (((ν ≫ J.blowupMap) ⁻¹ᵁ U : S.Opens) : Set S) ∧
        (∃ hη : (ν ≫ J.blowupMap) (genericPoint S) = genericPoint S₀,
          Function.Bijective
            (S₀.presheaf.stalkSpecializes (specializes_of_eq hη) ≫
              (ν ≫ J.blowupMap).stalkMap (genericPoint S) :
                S₀.functionField ⟶ S.functionField)) ∧
        (∃ (σB : H →* Aut J.blowup) (σS : H →* Aut S),
          (∀ h : H, (σB h).hom ≫ J.blowupMap = J.blowupMap ≫ (σ₀ h).hom) ∧
          (∀ h : H, (σS h).hom ≫ ν = ν ≫ (σB h).hom)) ∧
        ∃ d : S ⟶ I.blowup, d ≫ I.blowupMap = ν ≫ J.blowupMap := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
